WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

NOTE An Thanh Dang - HULK: Learning Whole-Body Forceful Loco-Manipulation for Humanoids.
Synced to 04_An_Thanh_Dang_HULK_CLEAN.mp4 (talk begins at 00:00:06.006 after the intro card).

1
00:00:08.066 --> 00:00:09.886
Hello everyone, my name is An.

2
00:00:10.886 --> 00:00:11.886
This is Arturo.

3
00:00:12.646 --> 00:00:14.126
Today we're excited to present HULK,

4
00:00:14.166 --> 00:00:18.086
learning whole-body forceful
loco-manipulation for humanoids.

5
00:00:20.066 --> 00:00:23.546
A lot of progress has been made
on agile dynamic whole-body controllers,

6
00:00:23.586 --> 00:00:26.886
where the humanoid can do really
cool things such as break dancing,

7
00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:30.266
back flips, kick flips, and parkour.

8
00:00:31.126 --> 00:00:34.846
But if you look closely, these
demonstrations have one thing in common.

9
00:00:35.246 --> 00:00:38.886
The humanoid is only moving itself
without any additional load.

10
00:00:40.906 --> 00:00:44.206
The utility of a humanoid
in an industrial setting isn't measured

11
00:00:44.246 --> 00:00:47.066
by the back flips it can perform,
but by the weight that it can handle.

12
00:00:47.366 --> 00:00:51.286
In this work, we focus on developing
a whole-body controller that enables

13
00:00:51.326 --> 00:00:52.526
forceful loco-manipulation,

14
00:00:52.566 --> 00:00:55.326
allowing humanoids
to carry heavy and bulky

15
00:00:55.366 --> 00:00:57.706
objects while maintaining
balance under load.

16
00:00:59.406 --> 00:01:02.466
Here we showcase a wide range
of impressive weight-bearing

17
00:01:02.506 --> 00:01:05.186
capabilities as demonstrated
in these videos.

18
00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:10.706
Our policy can do dynamic power clean,
it can carry a really heavy plate,

19
00:01:10.746 --> 00:01:14.286
it can do a farmer's walk,
and it can do a deadlift pretty stable.

20
00:01:14.626 --> 00:01:17.146
All while maintaining balance
under substantial loads.

21
00:01:19.346 --> 00:01:22.506
Our method is trained using
a three-stage teacher-student framework.

22
00:01:22.726 --> 00:01:25.626
We first train a whole-body
controller going

23
00:01:25.666 --> 00:01:28.206
through random wrist force perturbations.

24
00:01:28.366 --> 00:01:34.766
This is the wrist force teacher and the
second stage we train a grasp teacher,

25
00:01:34.806 --> 00:01:40.126
which performs locomotion while holding
a bunch of objects using its upper body,

26
00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:41.166
so a whole-body grasp.

27
00:01:42.786 --> 00:01:47.066
We distill both of these teachers,
trained with reinforcement learning,

28
00:01:47.106 --> 00:01:51.286
into a single proprioceptive student
policy that we can deploy onto hardware,

29
00:01:51.326 --> 00:01:52.866
and in this case, we train G1.

30
00:01:55.446 --> 00:01:58.486
During teacher training we use model
predictive control and a capture

31
00:01:58.526 --> 00:02:03.266
point control barrier function
to add auxiliary reward guidance.

32
00:02:09.706 --> 00:02:14.606
The rewards coming from the MPC helps
in force reasoning and through a reduced

33
00:02:14.646 --> 00:02:18.586
order model we are able to model the
forces and the risks and therefore plan

34
00:02:18.626 --> 00:02:24.266
accordingly, while the CVF rewards help us
in balance recovery under persistent load.

35
00:02:24.506 --> 00:02:28.946
Thanks to this, we're able to handle
up to 130 newtons of disturbance.

36
00:02:31.206 --> 00:02:36.326
We're very glad to showcase Hulk under
a variety of industrially relevant tasks.

37
00:02:37.286 --> 00:02:40.406
In the top left,
you can see the cart pulling task,

38
00:02:40.446 --> 00:02:44.706
in which you can see the robot pulling
the dolly by walking backwards.

39
00:02:45.286 --> 00:02:49.046
We're very happy to say that Hulk
can pull the dolly up to 300 kilograms

40
00:02:49.086 --> 00:02:54.786
which is 100 more kilograms than the best
performing baseline in this task.

41
00:02:56.226 --> 00:03:01.126
In the top right we see the robot
can handle around 13 kilograms of payload.

42
00:03:04.546 --> 00:03:10.046
The payload is mounted onto the wrists
and we can move in a straight line just

43
00:03:10.086 --> 00:03:14.726
by commanding the robot to move forward
and despite having these unmodeled

44
00:03:14.766 --> 00:03:17.066
masses in the upper body we can you
can see that it's moving straight.

45
00:03:18.486 --> 00:03:21.146
Finally, we have
the canister and fridge task.

46
00:03:21.726 --> 00:03:24.846
And the robot is holding
the object by pushing it against

47
00:03:24.886 --> 00:03:32.326
the body and performing a whole-body
grasp using the upper body.

48
00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:38.066
We are able to achieve a stable locomotion
despite these unmodeled objects.

49
00:03:38.326 --> 00:03:41.386
And you can see that even
canister will be held

50
00:03:41.426 --> 00:03:43.766
from the side and also
the fridge in front.

51
00:03:43.806 --> 00:03:46.926
So, we are not tied
to a specific orientation

52
00:03:46.966 --> 00:03:48.946
of the objects that we are addressing.

53
00:03:51.226 --> 00:03:55.126
To summarize, HOLD uses
MPC and CDF reward guidance

54
00:03:55.166 --> 00:03:58.606
and distills two teachers into
a single proprioceptive student.

55
00:04:00.326 --> 00:04:04.826
HOLD transfers zero-shot
to the Unitree G1 and has forceful

56
00:04:04.866 --> 00:04:08.526
loco-manipulation and whole-body
carry capabilities.

57
00:04:09.146 --> 00:04:12.646
Please scan the top right QR
code to visit our website,

58
00:04:12.686 --> 00:04:16.626
you will see table results
and more cool videos.

59
00:04:17.286 --> 00:04:24.066
And definitely come by our poster
at 161 so we can chat

60
00:04:24.106 --> 00:04:26.466
more about the specifics
and any questions that you have.

61
00:04:26.686 --> 00:04:27.686
Thank you very much.

62
